<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> snow tubing means sliding down groomed lanes on an inflatable tube, then riding a lift or moving carpet back up. Many ski resorts run tubing hills, and some standalone tubing parks do too. Tickets are often sold by timed session and can sell out on weekends and holidays, so book ahead, check age and height rules, and dress like you're going skiing.</p></div>

<h2>Why tubing is great for families</h2>
<p>Tubing needs no lessons, no rental gear beyond the tube, and almost no skill. Kids who are too young to ski, grandparents who don't ski, and visitors trying snow for the first time can all ride together. It's a popular add-on to a <%= link_to "family ski trip", guide_path("family-ski-trip") %>, especially for an evening activity or a day off the slopes.</p>

<h2>How to find snow tubing near you</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ski resorts:</strong> many run a separate tubing area near the base, often open in the afternoon and evening. Check the resort's website for a tubing or activities page.</li>
  <li><strong>Standalone tubing parks:</strong> some parks focus only on tubing and may make their own snow.</li>
  <li><strong>Small local ski hills:</strong> community hills often offer tubing with shorter drives and smaller crowds.</li>
  <li><strong>Search smart:</strong> look up "snow tubing" plus your nearest ski area or town, and check that the hill is open; snow conditions decide opening days.</li>
</ul>
<p>Browse <%= link_to "ski resorts by state and province", regions_path %> to find mountains near you, then check their activities pages for tubing.</p>

<h2>Tickets, sessions, and rules</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Timed sessions:</strong> many tubing hills sell tickets for a set block of time. Arrive early for check-in.</li>
  <li><strong>Book ahead:</strong> weekend, holiday, and evening sessions often sell out.</li>
  <li><strong>Age and height rules vary:</strong> some hills have a minimum height for riding alone, a separate area for small kids, or rules on whether adults can ride with children. Check before you go.</li>
  <li><strong>Waivers:</strong> many hills require a signed waiver, sometimes online in advance.</li>
  <li><strong>Lifts:</strong> most hills use a moving carpet or tow to pull you and your tube back up, so there's little uphill walking.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What to wear</h2>
<p>You'll sit and spin in snow, so waterproof layers matter more than you'd expect.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Waterproof jacket and snow pants or bibs.</strong> Jeans get soaked fast.</li>
  <li><strong>Warm layers underneath,</strong> with a base layer that isn't cotton. See <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Waterproof gloves or mittens,</strong> a warm hat, and a neck gaiter.</li>
  <li><strong>Insulated, waterproof boots</strong> with good traction for walking in snow. See <%= link_to "how to choose snow boots", guide_path("best-snow-boots") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Goggles or sunglasses</strong> for flying snow on fast lanes.</li>
  <li><strong>A helmet</strong> is a smart choice for kids, and some hills recommend or allow them.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Tubing safety</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Follow staff instructions</strong> and wait for the attendant's signal before starting down.</li>
  <li><strong>Ride feet first, sitting in the tube.</strong> Don't ride head first or standing.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep hands and feet in</strong> and hold the handles.</li>
  <li><strong>Clear the run-out quickly</strong> at the bottom so the next rider has space.</li>
  <li><strong>Walk up only on the designated path,</strong> never in the tubing lanes.</li>
  <li><strong>Only link tubes or ride together</strong> if the hill allows it.</li>
  <li><strong>Take warm-up breaks,</strong> especially with young kids, and watch for cold fingers and wet clothes.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Tubing vs sledding</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Tubing hills</strong> have groomed lanes, run-outs, staff, and lifts back up. They're built for this.</li>
  <li><strong>Sledding</strong> usually happens on public hills with no grooming, lifts, or staff. It's free but less controlled. Pick hills with a long, flat run-out away from trees, roads, fences, and water.</li>
  <li><strong>Speed:</strong> groomed tubing lanes can be fast; the lane walls keep riders on course.</li>
</ul>
